From: Angelo Arifi <arifilabs@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 25 Jul 2026 03:20:59 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] docs: make the fork buildable by someone who did not build it

A fresh-clone stranger test was run for real: clone, replay, configure and
build, using only what the repository says. Five blockers were reproduced.

1. The README's replay block could not work. It said to check out the upstream
   base and then `git am patches/series/*.patch` - but patches/ does not exist
   at the base commit, because the series is what adds it. Checking out the base
   deletes the files you are about to apply. Verbatim result:
   `fatal: could not open 'patches/series/*.patch' for reading`. Replaced with
   `series replay --onto`, which replays into its own worktree.

2. Nothing in the repository told anyone how to build it. There was no build
   section and no build document. Without `-D_WIN32_WINNT=0x0A00` a MinGW build
   fails at 262/346 with `'::CreateFile2' has not been declared` at
   vendor/cpp-httplib/httplib.cpp:1471 - an error naming neither flag nor fix,
   which cost this fork three builds. docs/BUILDING.md now states each flag with
   the failure it prevents, both verified from a clean clone.

3. `git clone` fails on Windows. Upstream carries a 161-character path under
   tools/ui/, so checkout dies with `Filename too long` unless core.longpaths is
   set. Upstream's path, but our first command.

4. The build recipe was unreachable from a fresh clone. arifi-recipe.cmake is
   gitignored and `recipe export` stops without a build directory - which you
   cannot configure without the recipe. `recipe export --live <snapshot>`
   already solved this and was documented as impossible; now documented, with
   the warning that 35 of the 310 entries are absolute paths into this box.

5. `licenses/README.md` was linked as `LICENSES/README.md` - a 404 on every
   case-sensitive filesystem, i.e. everywhere it would be published.

Stale counts corrected: 40 -> 62 patches and 40 -> 73 commits in README, 38 ->
62 and 309 -> 310 in the arifi-sync README.

Adds docs/HARDWARE-PROFILES.md, because a single default is wrong for whoever
it does not match. GGML_ARIFI_VNNI_REPACK is the live case: +327%/+442% prompt
CPU-only, -77%/-88% prompt on Vulkan. It ships OFF, which is right for this rig
and wrong for a CPU-only user, and that user had no way to find out. Runtime
auto-detection was considered and deliberately NOT implemented - it would make
ggml-cpu introspect sibling backends, "GPU present" is the wrong predicate, and
it would flip a measured default with no measurement. Named profiles and a
startup advisory are proposed instead, and recorded as unimplemented.

README gains an explicit scope statement: what this fork is, what it is not,
the one machine every number was measured on, and the platforms nobody has ever
built it on. OPTIONS-REGISTRY gains a CPU-only row and an "any other hardware"
row that promises nothing.

series check --ref master still PASSES (62 patches, byte-identical replay); no
master commit is touched and the series is unchanged.

Origin: ArifiLabs lane-110C public-readiness
Evidence: research/local-inference/trial-evidence/110/run-journal.jsonl
Measured-effect: documentation only; no engine behaviour changed. The CPU-only
 build (no Vulkan) was built and run from a fresh clone: llama-server reports
 version 10141 (bc626ba1e), built with GNU 14.2.0, empty device list.

